.Dd October 29, 2026
.Dt XFT_PALLOC 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_palloc ,
.Nm xft_palloc_reserve ,
.Nm xft_palloc_commit ,
.Nm xft_palloc_guard ,
.Nm xft_palloc_resize ,
.Nm xft_palloc_free
.Nd page allocator
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In alloc.h
.Ft t_buffer
.Fn xft_palloc "t_size size"
.Ft t_buffer
.Fn xft_palloc_reserve "t_size size"
.Ft t_result
.Fn xft_palloc_commit "t_any ptr" "t_size size"
.Ft t_result
.Fn xft_palloc_guard "t_any ptr" "t_size size"
.Ft t_buffer
.Fn xft_palloc_resize "t_buffer b" "t_size new_size"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_palloc_free "t_buffer b"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions take memory directly from the operating system: Linux uses
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Xr madvise 2 ,
.Xr mremap 2 ,
.Xr mprotect 2
and
.Xr munmap 2 ,
and Windows calls
.Ql VirtualAlloc ,
.Ql VirtualProtect
and
.Ql VirtualFree .
The arena, general-purpose and reporting allocators get their memory here, and
.Fn xft_page_allocator
wraps these functions in a
.Vt t_allocator ;
see
.Xr xft_alloc 3 .
The memory is private, anonymous and zero-filled.
.Pp
.Fn xft_palloc_reserve
reserves address space for
.Fa size
bytes, and
.Fn xft_palloc_commit
backs the
.Fa size
bytes at
.Fa ptr
with readable and writable memory.
.Fn xft_palloc
does both and releases the reservation if the commit fails.
.Fn xft_palloc_guard
makes the pages of a range inaccessible, so that any access to them faults.
.Fn xft_palloc_resize
grows or shrinks
.Fa b
to
.Fa new_size ,
moving it when needed, and
.Fn xft_palloc_free
releases
.Fa b .
.Ss Sizes
The
.Fa .size
of a returned buffer is the rounded size, and
.Fn xft_palloc_resize
and
.Fn xft_palloc_free
expect the buffer back exactly as returned.
.Pp
On Linux, a size of at most 1 MiB rounds up to the next power of two, and
to at least 8.
A larger size rounds up to a hugepage tier when the padding is at most an
eighth of the tier: 2 MiB, 8 MiB, 16 MiB, 256 MiB, 512 MiB, 1 GiB, 2 GiB and,
on 64-bit targets, 16 GiB.
Any other size rounds up to a multiple of 4096.
For example, 15 MiB rounds to the 16 MiB tier, and 17 MiB stays 17 MiB.
.Pp
On Windows, sizes round up to a multiple of 4096, and hugepages are not used.
.Ss Linux
.Fn xft_palloc_reserve
maps the memory readable and writable, so a reservation is usable at once and
.Fn xft_palloc_commit
does nothing.
The memory is mapped once with ordinary pages.
When the rounded size is a hugepage tier, the mapping is also marked with
.Xr madvise 2
and
.Dv MADV_HUGEPAGE ,
and the kernel decides whether and when transparent huge pages back it, either
at fault time or later through
.Ql khugepaged .
.Fn xft_palloc_reserve
ignores a failed
.Xr madvise 2 .
.Fn xft_palloc_resize
calls
.Xr mremap 2
with
.Dv MREMAP_MAYMOVE ,
and a remapped mapping keeps its
.Dv MADV_HUGEPAGE
mark.
When
.Xr mremap 2
fails,
.Fn xft_palloc_resize
allocates a new buffer, copies the smaller of the two sizes and frees the old
buffer.
.Fn xft_palloc_guard
calls
.Xr mprotect 2
with
.Dv PROT_NONE ,
so
.Fa ptr
must be aligned to 4096, and guarding part of a huge page splits it.
.Ss Windows
.Fn xft_palloc_reserve
reserves with
.Dv MEM_RESERVE ,
and nothing in the reservation is accessible until
.Fn xft_palloc_commit
commits it with
.Dv MEM_COMMIT
and
.Dv PAGE_READWRITE .
Both act on every page the range touches.
.Fn xft_palloc_guard
sets
.Dv PAGE_NOACCESS
with
.Ql VirtualProtect ,
which works on committed pages only; reserved pages that were never committed
fault on access anyway.
.Fn xft_palloc_resize
allocates a new buffer, copies the smaller of the two sizes and frees the old
buffer.
.Fn xft_palloc_free
releases the whole reservation with
.Dv MEM_RELEASE ,
whatever
.Fa b Ns .size
holds.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_palloc
and
.Fn xft_palloc_reserve
return a buffer whose
.Fa .mem
is
.Dv nullptr
on failure.
.Pp
.Fn xft_palloc_resize
returns
.Fa b
unchanged when the rounded
.Fa new_size
equals
.Fa b Ns .size ,
and a buffer with
.Fa .mem
set to
.Dv nullptr
and
.Fa .size
set to 0 on failure, in which case
.Fa b
remains valid.
.Pp
.Fn xft_palloc_commit
and
.Fn xft_palloc_guard
return
.Dv OK ,
or
.Dv KO
on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Place input flush against a guard page, so that reading one byte past it
faults:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
t_buffer	page;
t_u8		*end;

page = xft_palloc(8192);
if (page.mem == nullptr
	|| !xft_palloc_guard((t_u8 *)page.mem + 4096, 4096))
	xft_exit_process(1);
end = (t_u8 *)page.mem + 4096;
xft_memcpy(end - 3, "abc", 3);
/* end[-3] to end[-1] are readable; end[0] faults */
xft_palloc_free(page);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_alloc 3 ,
.Xr xft_arena 3 ,
.Xr xft_os 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
On Linux, transparent huge pages back a tier only when
.Pa /sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled
is
.Ql always
or
.Ql madvise .
A huge page takes all of its memory when the kernel installs it, so the padding
of a tier, up to an eighth of its size, may take real memory; with ordinary
pages only the pages that are touched take memory.
.Pp
On Linux,
.Fn xft_palloc_free
and
.Fn xft_palloc_resize
act on
.Fa b Ns .size
bytes, so a buffer with a different size unmaps or moves a different range.
